    summary: `Frederick Douglass did not give his most famous Independence Day speech on July 4. He spoke on July 5, 1852, in Rochester, New York, to an antislavery audience gathered by the Rochester Ladies' Anti-Slavery Sewing Society. That detail matters. Douglass was not rejecting the idea of liberty. He was forcing listeners to ask why a slaveholding nation celebrated freedom while millions were enslaved and while the Fugitive Slave Act made capture a national duty. The daily challenge names the speech. The encyclopedia page has to explain the setting, the audience, the strategy, the risk, and the reason the speech still cuts. Douglass made patriotism answer to evidence. He did not let national ritual become a hiding place.`,
